    concept:
      '`std::optional<T>` from `<optional>` holds either a value or nothing. It replaces the old conventions of returning `-1`, a null pointer, or a bool plus an output parameter.\n\nThe advantage is that the absence is **in the type**, so the compiler reminds you it exists. Test with `.has_value()` or by using the optional directly as a bool, read with `*opt` or `.value()`, and supply a fallback with `.value_or(default)`.\n\nUse it when failure is ordinary and expected \u2014 a lookup that finds nothing. Use an exception when failure means something has genuinely gone wrong.',
    example: '#include <iostream>\n#include <optional>\n#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n\nstd::optional<int> find_first_even(const std::vector<int>& v) {\n    for (int n : v) {\n        if (n % 2 == 0) return n;\n    }\n    return std::nullopt;\n}\n\nstd::optional<int> parse(const std::string& s) {\n    try {\n        return std::stoi(s);\n    } catch (...) {\n        return std::nullopt;\n    }\n}\n\nint main() {\n    auto found = find_first_even({3, 7, 8, 9});\n    std::cout << std::boolalpha << found.has_value() << \'\\n\';\n    if (found) std::cout << *found << \'\\n\';\n\n    auto missing = find_first_even({1, 3, 5});\n    std::cout << missing.has_value() << \'\\n\';\n    std::cout << missing.value_or(-1) << \'\\n\';\n\n    std::cout << parse("42").value_or(0) << \'\\n\';\n    std::cout << parse("abc").value_or(0) << \'\\n\';\n}',
